# A template of a script: `mise run ffi-test` copies `tree/` into a
# temporary directory, writes this file there with `@DIR@` replaced by that
# directory's absolute path, and has the editor's headless mode run it
# against the config in `../config/`, of which it loads nothing: there is
# no `reload`, so every command its keys run is one the editor ships. The
# run must exit with status 0 and print exactly `expected` once the
# temporary directory's path is written `@DIR@`, each scene's published
# count `@N@`, and the job lines are left out: a search's deliveries are
# what the library has found when the job reads it, so how many there are,
# and so how many job lines and scenes come of them, differs from run to
# run. It writes no file: the headless mode has no state directory here,
# so the working set is kept in memory alone. The directory has a
# fixed-length path under `/tmp`, as `open-file`'s has.
#
# It is a working set's range added from project search's results: the
# tree is a workspace by its marker `.project`; `needle` is in two of its
# files, `a.txt` and `b.txt`, and not in `c.txt`. A row of the results
# added to a working set is the file's range, not the results', so the set
# opened shows that row of `b.txt`, and visiting it shows `b.txt` at the
# row. It pins what project search and the working set do together,
# whatever carries each.

# `:e` opens `a.txt`; the load and the workspace's find are waited for.
key : Semicolon shift
commit e @DIR@/tree/a.txt
key <Enter> Enter -
join
scene

# `project-search` with no pattern asks for one, labelled with the
# workspace of the buffer it was called from; `needle` is searched, and the
# results show the excerpts of `a.txt` and `b.txt` in walk order, the
# search's matches drawn in `search-match`. `b.txt`, open in no buffer, is
# loaded by the search.
run project-search
join
scene
commit needle
key <Enter> Enter -
join
scene

# The cursor goes down to the results' row of `b.txt`'s match, the
# seventh, and `working-set-add` asks for a set; `found` is a new set, and
# the range it adds is `b.txt`'s third row, not the results' seventh.
key j KeyJ -
key j KeyJ -
key j KeyJ -
key j KeyJ -
key j KeyJ -
key j KeyJ -
scene
run working-set-add
join
scene
commit found
key <Enter> Enter -
join
scene

# `working-set-open` asks for the set and shows its buffer: the range is
# held in `b.txt`, so its row is the one excerpt, and none is drifted.
run working-set-open
join
commit found
key <Enter> Enter -
join
scene

# `RET` in the set's buffer visits the range: `b.txt` at its third row.
key <Enter> Enter -
join
scene
